  "summary": "Carved living trees, snarled and smiling — masks that heal by fright.",
  "description": "False Face Society (Ga':goh':ah) masks are carved living-face portraits of the Great Sneezing Wind Spirit's helping beings: crooked mouths, broken noses, bulging brows — each carved from a living basswood, maple or poplar so the spirit inhabits the wood, then painted red, black or white and hung with horsehair. Members of the medicine society wear them with torn shirts and turtle-rattle to chase sickness from longhouses in blowing-cure rituals. The masks are sacred, not decorative: museums returning them has become part of Native American repatriation ethics, and many Haudenosaunee ask that they never be photographed or sold.",
  "culturalContext": "False Face masks are living Haudenosaunee healing technology, not art objects: carved from living trees, they cure through the medicine society's blowing rites, and their repatriation ethics (many nations refuse museum display and photography) make the mask a touchstone of Indigenous sacred-property debates.",
